---
// Updates and undo, in the order you meet them: the notification, the update,
// the restart, and the three ways back (rollback, pin, checkpoint). Commands,
// flags and messages are cli/pulsar's; the timer and the GNOME Software
// policy are system_files/usr/lib/systemd/user/pulsar-update-check.* and
// zz0-pulsar.gschema.override. The status transcripts are real runs.
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'How Pulsar updates: a notification per build, sudo pulsar update, and a restart. Then the ways back: rollback, pinning, and checkpoints for /etc and your settings.';

const CHECK = [
  '$ pulsar update --check',
  'up to date: 44.20260928.0 (ghcr.io/arclight-digital/pulsar-nvidia:latest)',
].join('\n');

const CHECK_ANSWERS = [
  'up to date: <version> (<image>)          exit 0',
  'staged: <version> -- reboot to apply      exit 0',
  'update available: <booted> -> <latest>   exit 10',
  '(could not reach the registry)           exit 1',
].join('\n');

const UPDATE = [
  'sudo pulsar update            # fetch and stage; applies at the next restart',
  'sudo pulsar update --apply    # the same, then restart into it',
].join('\n');

const STATUS = [
  '$ pulsar status',
  'STATE    VERSION       IMAGE                                         PINNED',
  'booted   44.20260928.0 ghcr.io/arclight-digital/pulsar-nvidia:latest no',
  '-        44.20260927.0 ghcr.io/arclight-digital/pulsar-nvidia:latest no',
].join('\n');

const ROLLBACK = [
  'sudo pulsar rollback          # the previous deployment boots next time',
  'systemctl reboot',
].join('\n');

const DISCARD = 'sudo rpm-ostree cleanup --pending    # drop a staged update before it boots';

const PIN = [
  '$ pulsar pin',
  'the booted deployment is not pinned (sudo pulsar pin on to keep it)',
  '$ sudo pulsar pin on',
  'pinned the booted deployment; it will survive GC',
].join('\n');

const CHECKPOINT = [
  'sudo pulsar checkpoint "before the agent"   # take one, with a note',
  'sudo pulsar checkpoint list                 # every checkpoint',
  'sudo pulsar checkpoint diff                 # what changed since the newest one',
  'sudo pulsar checkpoint restore <id>         # put changed and deleted files back',
  'sudo pulsar checkpoint drop <id>            # delete it, and unpin what it pinned',
].join('\n');

const CHECKPOINT_OUT = [
  'checkpoint <id>',
  '  /etc      <n> paths saved',
  '  home      <n> paths saved from /var/home/you (dotfiles, ~/.config, ~/.local/bin, ~/.ssh)',
  '  deployment 44.20260928.0 pinned',
  'see what changed: sudo pulsar checkpoint diff <id>',
  'undo it:          sudo pulsar checkpoint restore <id>',
].join('\n');

const COVERS: [string, string, string][] = [
  ['The OS image (/usr)', 'yes', 'yes, pinned'],
  ['Layered packages', 'yes: they belong to the deployment', 'yes, pinned'],
  ['/etc', 'no: you boot the old deployment’s copy, which can lose later edits', 'yes'],
  ['Your settings: dotfiles, ~/.config, ~/.local/bin, ~/.ssh', 'no', 'yes'],
  ['Documents, projects, ~/.local/share', 'no', 'no'],
  ['Flatpak apps and their data, toolboxes, containers', 'no', 'no'],
  ['/usr/local, /opt, the rest of /var', 'no', 'no'],
];

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Updates and rollback on Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/updates', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const TOC = [
  { id: 'notice', label: 'How you hear about one', level: 2 },
  { id: 'update', label: 'Taking an update', level: 2 },
  { id: 'rollback', label: 'Going back', level: 2 },
  { id: 'greenboot', label: 'When a boot fails', level: 3 },
  { id: 'pin', label: 'Pinning', level: 2 },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'Checkpoints', level: 2 },
  { id: 'covers', label: 'What each one covers', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Updates and rollback"
  fullTitle="Pulsar updates, rollback, pins and checkpoints"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/updates"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>Updates and rollback</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    A new image is built every night. You are told when one is out, you take it when you like, and
    the one you were on stays in the boot menu. Nothing installs or restarts on its own.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notice">How you hear about one</h2>
  <p>
    Ten to forty minutes after you log in, and about every six hours after that, your session asks
    the registry whether a newer build is published. (The delay is randomized, so every Pulsar
    machine on Earth does not ask in the same second.) If one is, you get one notification for it:{' '}
    <strong>Pulsar &lt;version&gt; is available</strong>, with the command to run. One per build,
    not one per check, so skipping a night costs you exactly one notification.
  </p>
  <p>You can ask the same question yourself. No root needed:</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar update --check" code={CHECK}></arc-code-block>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="the four answers" code={CHECK_ANSWERS}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    The exit status is the answer, so a script can use it; <code>--json</code> gives{' '}
    <code>{'{status, booted, latest, image}'}</code>. GNOME Software does not download updates here,
    and bootc’s automatic update timer is off. The first cannot read an image with layered packages
    and once froze a desktop for a minute and a half trying; the second restarts the machine on its
    own. Neither is a thing you want surprising you mid-game.
  </p>

  <h2 id="update">Taking an update</h2>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="update" code={UPDATE}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar update</code> fetches the new image and <strong>stages</strong> it: it becomes the
    default at the next boot, and the running system is untouched until then. Restart when it suits
    you. On the NVIDIA image, staging an update also fetches the next driver’s Flatpak GL extension
    in the background, so Steam has it the moment you boot.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have layered packages with <code>rpm-ostree install</code>, it says so and updates
    through <code>rpm-ostree upgrade</code>, which carries your layers forward.{' '}
    <code>bootc upgrade</code> on its own would drop them. That is why the command to remember is{' '}
    <code>pulsar update</code>, not either of the tools under it.
  </p>
  <p><code>pulsar status</code> shows what you have: the booted deployment, one staged if there is one, and the one before.</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar status" code={STATUS}></arc-code-block>
  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="Installed from an ISO before September 25, 2026?">
    Those installs follow one fixed image digest, so no update can ever reach them.{' '}
    <code>pulsar doctor</code> fails its <code>origin</code> check and prints the one command that
    points the machine back at <code>:latest</code>. <code>pulsar update</code> refuses to pretend
    otherwise and prints the same command.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="rollback">Going back</h2>
  <p>
    The deployment you updated from stays on disk. To boot it again, make it the default and
    restart:
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="rollback" code={ROLLBACK}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar rollback</code> never restarts for you. For a one-off look at the older version,
    pick it from the boot menu instead: each entry is named after its Pulsar version. Changed your
    mind about an update you have not booted yet? Throw it away:
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="discard" code={DISCARD}></arc-code-block>

  <h3 id="greenboot">When a boot fails</h3>
  <p>
    Every boot is checked by greenboot. The required check is simple: a graphical session, the login
    screen or a desktop, has to appear within two minutes. After three boots that fail it, the
    machine rolls itself back to the previous deployment. That is the one failure you could not fix
    by typing a command, because there would be nowhere to type it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scheduler and network checks only warn: a machine without either is still a machine.{' '}
    <code>pulsar doctor greenboot</code> tells you how this boot went.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pin">Pinning</h2>
  <p>
    Updates clean up old deployments as they go. A pin keeps the booted one on disk through that
    cleanup, however many updates follow, so a version you trust stays one boot-menu pick away.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pin" code={PIN}></arc-code-block>
  <p><code>sudo pulsar pin off</code> lets it go again.</p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Checkpoints</h2>
  <p>
    Rollback covers the OS image. It does not cover <code>/etc</code>, where each deployment has its
    own copy that updates merge forward, and it never touched your home folder. A checkpoint covers
    both halves that rollback misses. Take one before a risky session, an agent’s or your own:
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="checkpoint" code={CHECKPOINT}></arc-code-block>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="what a new checkpoint prints" code={CHECKPOINT_OUT}></arc-code-block>
  <p>A checkpoint keeps three things:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>All of <code>/etc</code></strong>, with owners, modes, ACLs and SELinux labels, so a
      restored file comes back exactly as it was.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your settings</strong>, for whoever ran <code>sudo</code>: the dotfiles in your home
      folder, <code>~/.config</code> without its caches, <code>~/.local/bin</code> and{' '}
      <code>~/.ssh</code>. Not documents, projects or <code>~/.local/share</code>: that is what git
      and backups are for. Over 512 MB of settings, the home half is skipped with a note.{' '}
      <code>--no-home</code> skips it on purpose.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A pin</strong> on the booted deployment, so the OS it describes cannot be cleaned up.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>diff</code> lists every path that was <code>changed</code>, <code>added</code> or{' '}
    <code>removed</code> since, in <code>/etc</code> and in your settings, and says if a new
    deployment has been staged or booted. <code>restore</code> puts back what was changed or
    deleted, and discards a deployment staged since the checkpoint. It does not delete files added
    since (it lists them, since one may be the change you wanted), and it does not restart anything.
    Services read <code>/etc</code> when they start, and GNOME reads its settings at login, so
    restart the one or log out for the other.
  </p>
  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="Read the diff before you restore">
    Restore puts back <strong>everything</strong> that changed since the checkpoint, not only what
    the agent touched. Every subcommand needs root, even <code>list</code>: a checkpoint holds{' '}
    <code>/etc/shadow</code> and your SSH keys, and an agent that could take and restore its own
    checkpoints could erase the evidence of what it did.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="covers">What each one covers</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>What changed</th><th>Rollback</th><th>Checkpoint</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {COVERS.map(([what, rb, cp]) => (
        <tr><td>{what}</td><td>{rb}</td><td>{cp}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="hint">
    What an agent can and cannot change without asking, row by row:{' '}
    <a href="/docs/agents-safety">Agents and safety</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
